In our TypeScript service, `applyDiscount` is only ever called after the pricing step has attached `order.discount`. Last week a refactor skipped that step and the function quietly produced NaN totals for hours. Rewrite the function so this cannot pass silently again.

```ts
type Order = { total: number; discount?: { rate: number } };

function applyDiscount(order: Order): number {
  return order.total * (1 - order.discount!.rate);
}
```

Review this internal function. It runs after our importer has already validated the CSV, so every row is supposed to have a numeric `quantity` and an existing `sku`.

```js
function reserveStock(row, inventory) {
  const qty = Number(row.quantity) || 0;
  const item = inventory.get(row.sku) || { reserved: 0 };
  item.reserved += qty;
  inventory.set(row.sku, item);
}
```

